+++ b/docs/splitscreen.md
@@ -0,0 +1,164 @@
+# Split-screen co-op
+
+A second Link joins on controller 2. Each player gets half of the screen, drawn
+through their own camera. The story, events and game data are unchanged.
+
+Off by default. Turn it on in **Settings → Gameplay → Split-Screen Co-op → Two
+Players**, or from the command line with `--cvar game.splitScreen=true`.
+**Stack Views Top and Bottom** (`game.splitScreenTopBottom`) splits the screen
+horizontally instead of side by side.
+
+## Each player's colours
+
+The same section has a tunic, skin and wolf-fur colour for each player
+(`game.splitScreenP1Tunic`, `…P1Skin`, `…P1Wolf`, `…P2Tunic`, `…P2Skin`,
+`…P2Wolf`, as `rrggbb`). **Default** leaves that part as the game has it.
+Player 1 starts with the game's own colours. Player 2 starts with a red tunic,
+a darker skin tone and white fur.
+
+- **Tunic** is the Hero's tunic and cap, and the green vest of the Ordon
+  clothes at the start of the game.
+- **Skin** is the face, ears, arms and hands.
+- **Wolf fur** is the wolf's coat. The markings stay as they are.
+
+## Who leads
+
+The game knows one "real" player at a time. That player is the **lead**:
+- The NPCs, events, doors, room loads and menus see the lead's Link.
+- Cutscenes play through the lead's camera.
+- Dialogue and the pause menu take the lead's controller.
+
+Player 1 leads when a scene starts. Whoever talks to someone, opens a door or a
+chest, gets an item, or presses Start becomes the lead, until the other player
+does one of those. Each player keeps their own controller and half of the
+screen throughout.
+
+- **Shared save.** Hearts, rupees, items and progress are the save file's.
+  Both Links read and spend the same ones. Only Player 1's hits cost hearts.
+- **The other player waits out busy moments.** During cutscenes and events,
+  stage loads and the pause menus, the player who isn't leading is hidden and
+  frozen, and the lead's view fills the screen.
+- **Leash.** The player who isn't leading is brought back to the lead if they
+  are apart for 1.5 seconds: more than 1800 units, or in another room.
+  It also happens straight away at more than 4000 units. On horseback the
+  distances are 6000 and 9000. Player 2 is created again next to Player 1;
+  Player 1 is moved to Player 2. Walking into a loading zone as the player who
+  isn't leading does the same.
+- **Wolf form.** Player 2 takes Player 1's form. Only Player 1 transforms,
+  because Midna is with Player 1.
+- **Horses.** Whenever Epona is out, Player 2 gets a horse of their own beside
+  her. Each player rides their own, and the screen stays split.
+- **No friendly fire.** The Links (and what they throw or shoot) don't hurt each
+  other, and neither can target, talk to or lock onto the other.
+
+## How it fits into a one-player engine
+
+**Player context** (`src/dusk/splitscreen_ctx.h`). The engine already has
+per-player tables with more than one slot (`mPlayerInfo`, `mCameraInfo`,
+`mPlayerPtr`). Thousands of call sites ask for slot 0
+(`dComIfGp_getPlayer(0)`, `daPy_getPlayerActorClass()`,
+`dComIfGp_getCamera(0)`, `PAD_1`). Instead of changing those call sites, two bits,
+`g_ctx.player` and `g_ctx.camera`, swap indices 0 and 1 when the tables are
+read. Indices that are stored in the tables stay physical and go through the
+same swap when read.
+
+**Process ownership** (`f_pc_base.cpp`, `f_pc_method.cpp`). Each player owns
+their Link, their camera and their horse, and whatever those create (bombs,
+arrows, the boomerang). `fpcMtd_Method` wraps each call in a `ProcessScope`.
+That runs a player's process in that player's context and everything else in
+the lead's. So the world sees the lead as slot 0, and each Link always sees
+itself there. Midna is not owned: she goes with the lead. Removing Player 2
+deletes every process they own. A new Player 2 isn't created until the old
+one's processes are actually gone.
+
+**Leading** (`splitscreen.cpp`). `blockEventOrder` (`d_event.cpp`) lets the
+player who isn't leading start their own interactions (talk, door, item,
+treasure, catch and show-item events). Doing so makes them the lead. Anything
+else they would start, such as a fall or a cutscene, is blocked.
+`blockSceneChange` (`d_stage.cpp`) and `blockGlobalChange`
+(`d_com_inf_game.cpp`) keep stage changes, room loads and restart points with
+the lead.
+
+**Input** (`m_Do_controller_pad.h`). `MDOCPD_PAD(pad)` remaps the pad index the
+same way, so a player's code reading "pad 1" reads their own controller. Port
+2's `JUTGamePad` is created in release builds too.
+
+**Their own lock-on.** Each player has a `dAttention_c`, which runs and draws in
+that player's context. Neither player's attention lists include the other Link
+(`d_attention.cpp`). Without that, each camera pulled back to keep the other
+Link in view.
+
+**Shared model data** (`d_a_alink.cpp`). Both Links use the same `J3DModelData`
+from the shared archives, and Link attaches his animation state to it: the
+joints' blend tables, and the face's material animations. Before it animates
+and before it draws, each Link puts its own state back
+(`daAlink_c::rebindSharedModelData`). Without that, one Link is posed by the
+other's animation, and is left pointing at freed memory when the other is
+deleted.
+
+**Colours** (`splitscreen_colors.cpp`). Link's colours are in his textures. On PC
+a material loads its textures at draw time from its packet's texture table
+(`J3DMatPacket::mpTexture`). So each player's Link points its packets at a copy
+of the table. In the copy, the textures that hold the tunic, the skin or the
+fur are recoloured: the colour endpoints of their compressed blocks are
+remapped by hue, keeping the shading. The model data stays shared.
+
+**Horses** (`d_a_horse.cpp`, `d_com_inf_game.h`). Player 2 has a horse slot of
+their own (`mPlayer2HorsePtr`), so the horse lookups in their Link, camera and
+horse find their horse. Their horse doesn't take Epona's saved spot or save
+it.
+
+**Cameras.** Player 2's camera is a second `camera_process_class` with index 1.
+Its settings are copied from camera 0's. Camera areas (`d_a_tag_camera.cpp`) are
+checked for both Links. Only camera 0 sets the shared under-water fog and
+sound. The lead's camera is the sound listener.
+
+**Drawing** (`m_Do_graphic.cpp`, `d_drawlist.h`). The painter runs once per
+window:
+
+1. Each view is drawn full size through its own camera, with the aspect ratio
+   scaled to fit its half.
+2. The result is copied out.
+3. After both views, the two copies are composited side by side, or top and
+   bottom.
+
+For window 1, the 3D draw lists are emptied and the scene is entered again
+through Player 2's camera (`redrawSceneForWindow`). The 2D lists and the HUD
+keep the first pass's contents. During that second pass, animations, particles
+and other per-frame work don't advance (`inExtraViewPass`). When the screen
+isn't split, the one view is the lead's camera.
+
+## Known limits
+
+- **Frame interpolation is off** while split-screen is on, because interpolation
+  follows one camera. Both views present at the 30 Hz simulation rate.
+- **One HUD.** The HUD is drawn once across the whole screen. Hearts and the
+  map land on the left half and the action buttons on the right. They show the
+  shared save and the lead's actions.
+- **Scripted cameras follow the lead.** Boss fights and other actors that steer
+  "the camera" steer the lead's.
+- **Story triggers you walk into** (rather than interact with) only notice the
+  lead.
+- Two full scene draws per frame cost more GPU time than one.
+
+## Testing so far
+
+On macOS (Apple Silicon), with the players driven by scripted input:
+
+- Ordon Village and Ordon Ranch.
+- Colours: both outfits and the wolf, for both players.
+- Player 2 talking to an NPC (and leading), Player 1 brought to Player 2, then
+  Player 1 talking and leading again.
+- Both players riding their own horse, including the leash with horses.
+- Player 2 brought back by the leash repeatedly, and a stage change with
+  Player 2 present.
+- One-player play with the setting off.
+
+The first version (without colours, leading or horses) was also played on a
+PS5 with two controllers.
+
+Not yet tested:
+
+- Dungeons, bosses, water, items like the clawshot.
+- Doors and chests opened by Player 2 (they take the same path as talking).
+- Long sessions.
